{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789459155,"nonce":"seEv2pk5t3JB2QVVY4jB9fS6jzI=","title":"String plugin: the hot primitives decoded UTF-8 and allocated twice","message":"`$String$equals`, `$String$compare`, `$String$concat`, `$String$from_int`, `$String$bytes` and `$String$join_n` went through `string_as_str`, which validates the bytes as UTF-8 on every call, and built a Rust `String` (`format!`, `to_string`, `String::with_capacity`) before copying it into the plugin string: two allocations and a validation pass per operation. On bench_tokenize `str(i % 100)` alone was 40 ms of 300k calls and `from_utf8` showed in every profile of a dict keyed by strings, since `zb_any_eq` on two strings is `$String$equals`.\n\nFix: equality and comparison over the bytes (the SDK already had a byte-wise `string_equals`; byte order is code point order for UTF-8), concat and join_n into one allocation of the final length, from_int through a stack buffer, bytes without decoding but refusing a cut inside a character.\n\nAfter: `str(i % 100)` 300k in under 10 ms; bench_records exec 379 to 301 ms (its key strings compare on every lookup); tokenize unchanged at 146 ms, where the time is now spread over building, splitting and counting.","files":null}]}